Q: Count 1-bits of an integer as fast as GCC&#39;s __builtin__popcount(int) A: <blockquote>
<p>I write a algorithm (taken from &quot;The C Programming Language&quot;) that counts the number of 1-bits very fast:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't see why anyone would characterize your approach as &quot;very fast&quot;.  It's a bit clever, and it should be faster on average than naive alternatives.  It also does not depend on the width of the representation of <code>int</code>, which is a plus.  I observe that it has undefined behavior for negative arguments, but that's a common theme for bitwise operators and functions.</p>
<p>Let's analyze, supposing a non-negative argument:</p>
<pre><code>int c = 0;
for (; n; ++c)
    n &amp;= n - 1;
</code></pre>
<ul>
<li><p>How many loop iterations are performed?</p>
<p>1 for each 1 bit in the binary representation of the value, irrespective of <em>where</em> in the value each bit lies</p>
</li>
<li><p>How much work is performed per iteration</p>
<ul>
<li>one increment of <code>c</code></li>
<li>one comparison of <code>n</code> against zero (plus one more of these when breaking out of the loop)</li>
<li>one decrement of <code>n</code> by 1</li>
<li>one bitwise 'and'</li>
</ul>
<p>That ignores reads and stores, which very likely can be made free or especially cheap by keeping the operands in registers.  If we assume equal cost for each of those, that's four operations per iteration.  For random 32-bit integers, there will be an average of 16 iterations, for a total of <strong>65 operations on average</strong>.  (Best case is just one operation, but worst is 129, which is no better than a naive implementation).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><code>__builtin_popcount()</code>, on the other hand, uses <strong>a single instruction</strong> regardless of input on platforms that support it, such as yours very likely is.  Even on those that don't have a for-purpose instruction, however, it can be done faster (on average).</p>
<p>@dbush has presented one such mechanism that has similar advantages to the one you present.  In particular, it does not depend on a pre-chosen integer width, and although it does depend on <em>where</em> in the representation the 1 bits reside, it does run faster for some arguments (smaller ones) than others.  If I'm counting right, that one will average <strong>around 20 operations</strong> on random 32-bit inputs: five in each of four loop iterations (only 0.4% of random inputs would require fewer than four iterations).  I'm counting one table read per iteration there, which I assume can be served from cache, but which is probably still not as fast as an arithmetic operation on values already held in registers.</p>
<p>One that is strictly computational would be:</p>
<pre><code>int count1BitsFast(uint32_t n) {
    n = (n &amp; 0x55555555u) + ((n &gt;&gt; 1) &amp; 0x55555555u);
    n = (n &amp; 0x33333333u) + ((n &gt;&gt; 2) &amp; 0x33333333u);
    n = (n &amp; 0x0f0f0f0fu) + ((n &gt;&gt; 4) &amp; 0x0f0f0f0fu);
    n = (n &amp; 0x00ff00ffu) + ((n &gt;&gt; 8) &amp; 0x00ff00ffu);
    n = (n &amp; 0x0000ffffu) + ((n &gt;&gt;16) &amp; 0x0000ffffu);
    return n;
}
</code></pre>
<p>That's pretty easy to count: five additions, five shifts, and ten bitwise 'and' operations, and 5 loads of constants for a total of <strong>25 operations</strong> for every input (and it goes up only to 30 for 64-bit inputs, though those are now 64-bit operations instead of 32-bit ones).  This version is, however, intrinsically dependent on a particular size of the input data type.</p>

Q: How to efficiently check whether a half-precision value is finite, in CUDA? A: <p>(Edited following <code>njuffa</code>'s answer.)</p>
<p><strong>tl;dr</strong>: The fastest methods seems to be the straightforward comparison of the exponent bits in the <code>unsigned short</code> representation of the half-precision value against the all-ones pattern.</p>
<h3>There are multiple methods to consider</h3>
<p>So far, between the lot of us, there seem to be 5 suggested approaches to checking the finity/finiteness of a half-precision value:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>(due to @njuffa) By IEEE 754, a number is finite if and only if it compares smaller with positive-infinity. All we now need is to get the positive-infinity value - which we can get either via <code>__ushort2half()</code> from the known integral representation, or by plain casting of the <code>INFINITY</code> macro:</p>
<pre><code>bool isfinite(half x) { return __habs(x) &lt; ((half)INFINITY); }
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>(due to @njuffa) CUDA offers an intrinsics for checking finiteness of <code>float</code> values; so let's just cast to <code>float</code> and use it.</p>
<pre><code>bool isfinite(half x) { return isfinite(__half2float(x)); }
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>(due to @wim) If we consider the bottom 15 bits (i.e. ignore the sign) of the representation as an integral value, the top 5 of those need to be all-ones; so the finite numbers are smaller than the non-finite ones.</p>
<pre><code>bool isfinite(half x) {
    unsigned short shifted_plus_infinity_rep = 0b0111110000000000u &lt;&lt; 1;
    unsigned short shifted_input_rep = __half_as_ushort (x) &lt;&lt; 1;
    return shifted_input_rep &lt; shifted_plus_infinity_rep;
}

</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Straightforward examination of the exponent bits: Non-finite values have an all-ones exponent; finite have something else.</p>
<pre><code>bool isfinite(half x){
    auto exponent_bits_mask = 0b111110000000000u;
    return (__half_as_ushort(x) &amp; exponent_bits_mask) != exponent_bits_mask;
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>Examination of the exponent bits via a bit field (essentially the same as the previous method)</p>
<pre><code>bool isfinite(half x){
    union destructured_half {
        half value;
        struct {
            unsigned short mantissa: 10;
            unsigned short exponent : 5;
           unsigned short sign : 1;
        } components;
    };
    destructured_half d { .value = x };
    return d.components.exponent != 0b11111u; // all ones
}
</code></pre>
</li>
<li><p>(@ericpostpischil's idea) Use self-subtraction. Only finite values yield <code>0</code> when subtracted from themselves (for infinity and NaN values, the result is NaN). This method has the aesthetic appeal of having the most terse code.</p>
<pre><code>bool isfinite(half x){
    return x - x == (half) 0;
}
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
<h3>Compilation results examined</h3>
<p>Adapted from <a href="https://godbolt.org/z/MEvG55E68" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> (GodBolt.org, CUDA 13.2, SM 89 target).</p>
<p>Boilerplate, signature &amp; final store instruction is skipped. The load instructions <em>should</em> be common - but they actually are not! So only removing the common <code>R1</code> setting.</p>
<p>Method 1:</p>
<pre><code> LDC.U16 R0, c[0x0][0x168] 
 MOV R2, c[0x0][0x160] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R3, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x164] 
 ULDC.64 UR4, c[0x0][0x118] 
 HSETP2.GEU.AND P0, PT, |R0|.H0_H0, +INF , +INF , PT 
 SEL R5, RZ, 0x1, P0 
 STG.E.U8 [R2.64], R5 
</code></pre>
<p>Method 2:</p>
<pre><code> HADD2.F32 R0, -RZ, c[0x0] [0x168].H0_H0 
 MOV R2, c[0x0][0x160] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R3, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x164] 
 ULDC.64 UR4, c[0x0][0x118] 
 FSETP.GEU.AND P0, PT, |R0|, +INF , PT 
 SEL R5, RZ, 0x1, P0 
 STG.E.U8 [R2.64], R5 
</code></pre>
<p>Method 3:</p>
<pre><code> LDC.U16 R0, c[0x0][0x168] 
 MOV R2, c[0x0][0x160] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R3, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x164] 
 ULDC.64 UR4, c[0x0][0x118] 
 IMAD.SHL.U32 R0, R0, 0x2, RZ 
 LOP3.LUT R0, R0, 0xffff, RZ, 0xc0, !PT 
 ISETP.GE.U32.AND P0, PT, R0, 0xf800, PT 
 SEL R5, RZ, 0x1, P0 
 STG.E.U8 [R2.64], R5 
</code></pre>
<p>Method 4:</p>
<pre><code> LDC.U16 R0, c[0x0][0x168] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R2, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x160] 
 ULDC.64 UR4, c[0x0][0x118] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R3, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x164] 
 LOP3.LUT R0, R0, 0x7c00, RZ, 0xc0, !PT 
 ISETP.NE.AND P0, PT, R0, 0x7c00, PT 
 SEL R5, RZ, 0x1, !P0 
 STG.E.U8 [R2.64], R5 
 EXIT 
</code></pre>
<p>Method 5:</p>
<pre><code> LDC.U16 R0, c[0x0][0x168] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R2, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x160] 
 ULDC.64 UR4, c[0x0][0x118] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R3, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x164] 
 LOP3.LUT R0, R0, 0x7c00, RZ, 0xc0, !PT 
 ISETP.NE.AND P0, PT, R0, 0x7c00, PT 
 SEL R5, RZ, 0x1, !P0 
 STG.E.U8 [R2.64], R5 
</code></pre>
<p>Method 6:</p>
<pre><code> LDC.U16 R0, c[0x0][0x168] 
 MOV R2, c[0x0][0x160] 
 IMAD.MOV.U32 R3, RZ, RZ, c[0x0][0x164] 
 ULDC.64 UR4, c[0x0][0x118] 
 HADD2 R0, R0.H0_H0, -c[0x0] [0x168].H0_H0 
 HSETP2.NEU.AND P0, PT, R0.H0_H0, RZ.H0_H0, PT 
 SEL R5, RZ, 0x1, P0 
 STG.E.U8 [R2.64], R5 
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>HADD2.F32</code> in method 2 is super weird! It adds the half-precision value to minus-zero. Why?</p>
<h3>Performance comparison</h3>
<h4>Benchmarking methodology</h4>
<p>I've followed @njuffa's approach to writing a micro-benchmark kernel: Cycle over all <code>unsigned short</code> values, treat each of them as a half-precision value, and check its finiteness. Different threads begin at different <code>unsigned short</code> values. The kernel is scheduled multiple times. The overhead of just running a  kernel with the same block &amp; grid dimensions, without the finity check logic, is  negligible - a few microseconds; so we ignore its effect.</p>
<p>I timed differently than @njuffa though:</p>
<ul>
<li>by using CUDA events rather than the system time. I also used cuda-api-wrappers.</li>
<li>by taking the mediam of 10 invocations of the kernel rather than the minimum.</li>
<li>I ran 100 iterations</li>
</ul>
<p>(If you believe these are problematic choices, say so in the comments please.)</p>
<h4>Results</h4>
<p>On an NVIDIA RTX 4600:</p>
